fn generate_github_alternative_url(url: &str) -> Option<(ureq::Agent, String)> {
  if !url.starts_with("https://github.com/") {
    return None;
  }

  generate_github_mirror_url_from_template(url)
    .or_else(|| generate_github_mirror_url_from_base(url))
    .map(|_alt_url| {
      (
        ureq::Agent::config_builder()
          .user_agent(BUNDLER_USER_AGENT)
          .build()
          .into(),
        url.to_owned(),
      )
    })
}

findings.body:
In `generate_github_alternative_url`, the mirror URL is produced by `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template(url)` or `generate_github_mirror_url_from_base(url)`, but the closure now binds it as `_alt_url` and returns `url.to_owned()`. The result is that whenever `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR` or `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR_TEMPLATE` is set, the bundler still downloads from github.com. Users behind firewalls, or in regions where GitHub is blocked, who configured a mirror to make builds work will now see the download fail.

The mirror branch also builds its agent with only the user agent. It drops the `proxy(ureq::Proxy::try_from_env())` and the `platform-certs` TLS config that `base_ureq_agent()` applies. So even if the URL were correct, mirror users behind a corporate proxy or with a custom CA would lose connectivity.

Nothing in the diff explains this, and the changelog only describes the user-agent enhancement. The existing tests only cover `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template`, so this regression is not caught.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="52-60"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The `_alt_url` binding is the tell: the computed URL is unused. The likely intent was to return the mirror URL and use `base_ureq_agent()`-style config, or to set the user agent on the base agent and return `alt_url`.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="49-62"}
fn generate_github_alternative_url(url: &str) -> Option<(ureq::Agent, String)> {
  if !url.starts_with("https://github.com/") {
    return None;
  }

  generate_github_mirror_url_from_template(url)
    .or_else(|| generate_github_mirror_url_from_base(url))
    .map(|alt_url| (base_ureq_agent(), alt_url))
}

`crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json` now reports `"tauri": "2.9.3"`, but the `tauri` crate in `crates/tauri/Cargo.toml` is still `2.9.2`, and this PR's change files do not include any `tauri` entry. The metadata field is used by the CLI as the expected core version for compatibility checks, so this points at a version that is not published from this tree. Either the `tauri` bump was intended and its manifest and changelog are missing, or the field should stay at `2.9.2`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json" lines="1-9"}

I'm not certain how the release tooling intends this field to be set, so this is a question for the author rather than a confirmed bug.
